'use strict';

/**
 * Every Modbus register the Huawei documentation defines, per device.
 *
 * This is a reference list, not a polling list. lib/modbus-registers.js holds the registers
 * the app actually reads and writes, roughly ninety of them; this file holds all the ones
 * the documentation describes, so the Registers tab can answer "what else is in there"
 * rather than only showing what we happen to use.
 *
 * WHERE EACH LIST COMES FROM
 *   inverter, battery, meter
 *     Parsed out of SUN2000MA V100R001C00SPC177 Modbus Interface Definitions, section 3
 *     (3.1 Inverter, 3.2 Battery, 3.3 Meter), then checked row by row against the
 *     specification's own tables. Those tables agree on every field: 112 of 112 inverter
 *     rows, 157 of 158 battery rows and 22 of 22 meter rows. The single exception is
 *     37000, where the document prints a footnote marker inside the name
 *     ("[Energy storage unit 1] [1] Running status") that is dropped here, because a
 *     footnote reference in a register list reads as a sub-index and is not part of the
 *     signal name.
 *
 *   sdongle
 *     Not in that document at all — the SDongle is specified separately, and this list is
 *     its reference table read straight. Note that it reuses addresses the inverter also
 *     defines: 30015 is the dongle's own serial number, 31200 its registration key.
 *
 *   emma
 *     Separate again, and shared by the three drivers that read one EMMA: the inverter, the
 *     battery and the power meter behind it are three Homey devices but one Modbus unit with
 *     one register map. 40000 is the ESS control mode here and the system time on a SUN2000,
 *     which is the whole reason these lists are per driver.
 *
 *   charger
 *     The smart charger's own table. Its registers overlap the EMMA's numerically and mean
 *     something else entirely — 30500 is the charger's phase A voltage — which is why it
 *     gets its own list rather than the EMMA's.
 *
 * Both the EMMA list and the charger list end with the five registers at 65500-65534, which
 * the documentation describes as a unified interface the EMMA provides for every device
 * behind it: alarm sequence numbers, device serial, alias and connection status.
 *
 * Not here: the EMMA smart meter's table at 30500-30550. No driver reads that unit — the
 * powermeter_emma_modbus driver reads the EMMA's own meter block from 31639 upward, on a
 * different unit id — and on the charger's unit 30500 means the phase A voltage instead.
 * Attaching one device's table to another's unit is the worst mistake this list could make.
 *
 * Fields per row:
 *   address       the Modbus register address
 *   length        how many 16-bit words it occupies (the Quantity column)
 *   type          the decoder in lib/modbus-client.js, or null where the documented type is
 *                 not a scalar this app can decode (MLD/Bytes, MULTIDATA and the like). A
 *                 null type means the row is listed but cannot be read into a value.
 *   specType      the type exactly as the documentation writes it, so the list stays checkable
 *   label         the signal name as the documentation writes it
 *   unit          the documented unit, blank where it says N/A
 *   decimalPower  -log10(Gain): what the raw value must be scaled by to land in `unit`
 *   conflict      present only where the documentation contradicts itself about a register,
 *                 which today means a type needing more words than the Quantity column
 *                 gives it. Such a row is listed but has no `type`, so nothing offers a
 *                 read that could only fail.
 *   rw            RO, RW or WO
 *
 * Generated rather than typed. See test/modbus-spec-registers.test.js.
 */

